They became a company in like 1981, 82 or something like that, and that's about how old this game is.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:20.360
This game was released on computers first and foremost and is now just like part of this wave that we're getting recently of just,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.180
well, I feel like this can be on the NES because anything can be on the NES now.

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:28.020
And that's exactly what happened here.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.620
Mule is a resource management game.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:41.440
The core gameplay revolves around managing three different types of resources on a newly colonized planet where you basically have to manage buying,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:47.320
selling, and trading goods, the goods being food, energy, and smithor.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:54.120
And it plays in a bit of a board game-like fashion.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:03:01.920
It kind of reminds me of the kind of board games, Joe, that we were playing during our board game nights a few years ago.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.240
Absolutely. I was thinking the same thing while I was playing, and I did not get to, usually I can scrap together a crew to play multiplayer.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.960
I was not able to this week.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:19.500
But the whole time I was playing this against a computer, I was like, this would be so much better with like three other people and like playing it like a board game.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.900
Yeah, and we'll talk about why, but I agree with you completely.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:29.500
This game is, even though you can play with three other computers, you have to play with four players, by the way.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.460
There's no like just 1v1 or anything like that.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.920
Yes, there is. Oh, there is?

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.940
Yeah. So I just said I couldn't get people.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.540
I did. My girlfriend and I did try it at first, and after like a minute, my girlfriend was like, I don't think this is my type of game.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.660
And I was like, you know, okay.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:49.160
Like I said an episode earlier, that she's not a big video game person.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:55.060
So this is probably like the most boring thing that she could just jump into for her second ever video game.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.200
I feel like that's so interesting, though.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.080
We should really talk about what the game is.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:06.140
But like, I feel like that's so interesting because that makes me question like a lot of the strategy elements when you just have two players playing.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.740
But of course, let's talk about the game.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:14.360
It is a game that works on a turn-based economy.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.360
So basically, one by one, players make their decisions.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:43.640
And the decisions that you make in the game revolve around owning plots of land and producing goods on those land in order to survive seasons and then use your surplus or rather the resources you no longer have to buy, trade, and sell these goods with other players.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:53.560
To accumulate a score where the player with the highest score at the end of the game, aka the one who was able to best manage all three of their resources appropriately, will win the game.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.880
So the way it works is you first select a plot of land.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:06.820
And the way that happens is a grid of a map moves in square boxes from left to right.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.780
And any player at any time can say, I want this piece of land.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.980
And then they own it.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.880
And they can't buy another plot of land in the same turn.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:21.980
So it becomes a race to make sure you get a good plot of land, like a mountain or a river.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:34.480
You want to be in a good location and preferably probably near the general store too because you'll have to walk to and from the general store to your plot of land.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.760
Joe, let's pause here for a second.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:38.620
Selecting the plots of land.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:41.620
You know, did you have a strategy for this?

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:46.420
Did you have a thought process that went into like, you know, how you were developing your land?

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.480
Well, so as far as which plot I select, yes.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:59.820
I mean, so like you said, you know, you want to pick a good plot, like whether you're near a river or you want to be near water if you're going to farm.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.420
You want to be near in the mountains if you're going to mine.

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:07.460
And you want to be on the plains if you're going to try and generate energy.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:14.320
Now, I'm not sure exactly how those affect your output.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:21.220
Like if I were to get a plot of land in the mountains and try to farm, how much less stuff I would get or how that would work.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:25.100
But intuitively, it seems like that's what you want.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:31.620
And, you know, secretly, I think that that's like kind of all there is to selecting what to select.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.560
It's like there's three types of land.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:34.980
There's three types of resources.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.540
And one coincides like one to one with each other.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:54.700
But then there's also this like and I don't have a strategy nailed down here, but there is something interesting about the way that you select where it the computer just shows you like here's plot one.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.320
A here's plot one beat one.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:06:59.900
See one. You know, it just goes right down the line and you're selecting.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.180
So like there is something.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:09.000
There's something there that's strategic that I haven't fully locked down yet about.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:16.580
Like, do you select something early or do you wait until everybody else has selected, see what they've done and now there's nothing they can do.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:22.500
And now you can select what you want based on what everybody else has.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:23.880
You know, that's that.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.080
So like that part, that's an interesting gameplay element that you wouldn't be able to really do in a board game.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.760
I mean, I guess there's probably some way you could figure it out.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.580
But this is like it's reliant on like you have one second to select this plot of land before.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.180
Like, OK, no one's buying this.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.700
We're on to the next plot.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.980
So, yeah, I think that there's a lot of potential for strategy there.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.820
It's hard to really come up with strategy when you're planning as three NES AI computer characters rather than other players.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:08:05.500
And it's interesting because the strategy is one consistently throughout this game that as each turn goes and turn would refer to all four players or however many players you have each completing their own turn.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:09.000
When you get to the next like selection of land plots again.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.440
Well, now you can't select four tiles that were previously selected.

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:16.360
Those are all owned. And so you have less to choose from.

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.680
And by the end of the game, you only have, you know, like the the only choices remaining.

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:28.300
So it kind of becomes a game of lockout where you say like, OK, well, this person desperately needs food.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:33.120
And this is the only food tile left, you know, like the only one that would produce near the river.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.320
So I'm going to make sure I grab this.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:55.800
What's interesting about that, of course, is that if everybody needs food and everybody wants the the land that's still directly on the river, the AI in the game, not even the computer players you play against, but just the computer itself will give it automatically to whoever needs it the most.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.140
Now, I find that very interesting.

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:09.960
All ties and in any in any area of like choosing at the same time or selling off things, the computer always gives it to whoever is deemed to need it the most.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:11.360
What do you think about that, Joe?

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:30.180
I mean, my gut reaction to that is I don't like it because it takes away kind of like your ability to monopolize something or to, you know, to to really use this economic system that's in this game to your advantage.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:38.180
But then the more I think about it, I do see the the merits of it also, because like what we're talking about right now is just selecting the plot of land.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:51.720
And that like if there's just one plot of land that that you two of you need, I can see them wanting to do something where it doesn't just come down to whoever has the fastest reflexes is going to be the like that's not this kind of game.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:10:04.760
So it's not like it's it's it's maybe not a perfect way to do it, but it's I think it's trying to stop people who are just quicker from just like kind of bullying the person that's just not as fast on the reflexes.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:11.320
And after every player selects their plot of land, then you go into your individual turns.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.060
And this is where a timer suddenly appears on the screen.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:23.360
It's basically just a bar that depletes like an hourglass and you have to do everything you want to do within that timer.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:29.180
If the timer runs out, you won't be able to do whatever remaining tasks you have.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:35.640
So anything you want to do in the game has to happen on this particular turn has to happen within that timer.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:43.940
More than likely, the most important thing you're going to do is you're going to go buy a mule, a M-U-L-E.

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:50.220
If you remember, that is one of those multiple use labor elements in this game.

00:10:50.220 --> 00:11:00.160
In the game, not so much, but in other iterations of a mule on like the computer, they look exactly like Star Wars AT-ATs.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.400
Really? Yeah, like like that's interesting.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:08.580
And OK, because not sure why that's allowed, but it's kind of funny that like EA still works with Star Wars.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:16.220
Yeah, maybe this is like what LucasArts was like, OK, that's that means that they're they know Star Wars like this.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:21.420
That was like their like their interview is like, oh, can you make can you make an AT-AT or AT-ST?

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.680
Which one? And they're like, oh, yeah, they made these AT-STs before.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:28.540
Maybe let's let's give them let's give them the Star Wars IP.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:34.780
Right. And so you buy your mule and you do that in the store.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:36.960
So you're actually now walking around as a character.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:42.580
You go in there, you buy your mule and then you have to decide, well, what's this mule going to do?

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:46.600
Is this mule going to generate food, energy or smith ore?

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:49.620
And you pay a fee for that as well.

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:54.700
And then you take it out of the shop and you're now on that overworld map screen again.

00:11:54.700 --> 00:12:04.400
And you walk on over to the plot of land you just bought and you say, OK, this will now be where I'm going to place this mule here and we're going to generate food here.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:10.600
Then you're going to leave that plot of land, go back to the general store and probably just go into the pub.

00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:12.700
The pub is the way to end your turn.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.300
It's an automatic way to win money.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.280
It depends on how the more time remaining is the more money you win.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.960
Oh, I didn't even realize that the time correlated to that, but that makes so much sense now.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.440
So, so, you know, obviously it's just like I did everything I need to do and I'm going to end my turn.

00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:29.880
You can go wampus hunting.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.820
Did you go wampus hunting at all, Joe?

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.960
I did not go wampus hunting.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.340
Tell me about it. It's a fool's errand, OK?

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:50.580
Because any kind of money that you might get out of this will not benefit you in the long run because you have a higher potential to waste your time and run out of time doing anything.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:58.360
And you need money to sell goods, to buy goods in this game, of which, of course, allow you to win the game.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:03.580
So why wouldn't you just finish your turn rather than go try to catch a wampus?

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:09.520
Like, sure, the payout is higher if you do catch the wampus, but it's not easy to do.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:17.820
Right. I wonder if there is some, like, reason if you're, like, in the deep strategy of this game, if there's ever a reason to do the wampus hunting.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:21.620
Maybe there's, like, a different play style that we haven't thought of or something.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:28.160
Also, speaking of wampus hunting, have we mentioned that this is an alien planet and you play as a bunch of aliens?

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.720
I just want to make sure people are clear on that.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:34.400
No, because we didn't mention all the alien characters that you play.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:39.520
Yeah, I'm sure we'll get into that. As we're talking about this, I just want to make sure it's not, like, yeah, we're not, like, just, like, on Earth here.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.600
Yeah, yeah, this isn't, we're not playing civilization.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:46.400
Yeah, yeah. We're not about to build the Roman Empire again, but this time with George Washington.

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:48.960
Yeah. I never played.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:59.520
And then there's also, at the beginning of your turn, there's a chance for a random event to happen, like acid rain or pests can take over.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:06.960
Pest attacks, yeah. Yeah. I think there's not earthquakes there, there's planet quakes, which sound bigger.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:17.920
Like, earthquakes, I never put together that they were talking about, like, the entirety of the Earth, but when you call it a planet quake, now it sounds like literally the entire planet is shaking.

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:19.940
Yeah, right. Which is violent.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:30.920
Well, what you're talking about now, too, these environmental things, that also informed my strategy of where to pick my plots of land.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:43.320
Because at one point I had, I don't remember exactly what it was, I might have had, like, a farm and an energy producing thing, or maybe it was two farms up in the north.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:54.860
And an acid rain storm came through, and it kind of goes in, like, a horizontal line across, like, one or two tiles.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:15:05.040
And having all my stuff in one area means that it's more likely to get hit by one, like, it's almost like you want to, you want to separate your stuff just to have contingencies.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.240
So, like, that factored in as well.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.880
Like, I didn't want, like, two farms right next to each other, which the river runs vertically, so I don't know why I would have done that anyways.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:25.680
But it's good to have things on, like, different sides of the, I don't know, if it's the whole planet or of the land, just to, like, to spread it out a bit.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:48.580
And after everyone has successfully completed whatever they want to do with the mules or just go right to the pub or try to catch a wampus, we head into the auction part of the game where it does show, like, how much you yielded in these different categories of, again, energy, food, and smith ore.

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:52.520
And then how much were depleted by the end of that season.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:57.240
And then basically, like, a goal line of, like, where you needed to be.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:16:01.940
And if you weren't there, it's time to start buying up some food.

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:06.200
If you weren't generating food, it's time to start buying.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.100
And if you had a lot of smith ore, it's time to start selling.

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:12.640
And this is the most interesting part of the game.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:18.340
This is also the crux of the game, so I understand why it's the most interesting part.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:27.500
Like, this is the only part that really involves any kind of interaction other than strategic blocking of other players' plots of land.

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.100
So in the auction, the way it works, you can decide if you're a buyer or seller.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:34.740
Sellers go up to the top of the screen.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.720
Buyers are at the bottom of the screen.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:38.960
And it goes one by one.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.580
So first with food, then energy, and then smith ore.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:43.180
I believe that's the order.

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:49.960
Anyway, the sellers are on a bar, which shows them, you know, like, let's say their current price.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:52.180
One food, $60.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:55.820
And the buyers are at the bottom, and they're saying one food, $10.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:17:00.480
The two bar graphs have to meet together.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:05.620
So in other words, the players have to meet in the middle in order to start trading.

00:17:05.620 --> 00:17:14.940
So if sellers have food that buyers desperately need, what's the incentive to come on down, right?

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:24.100
But if you have too much of a product that nobody else needs, what's the incentive of anybody to buy from you?

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:38.640
Yeah, it's really interesting the way they do it, because I can see if any kids got past the boredom of some of the other stuff that, like, a kid might have in this game, this kind of teaches you, like, some of the basics of, like, how, like, an economy works.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:39.740
Yeah, supply and demand.

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:47.480
Yeah, and, like, it truly is when you have a resource and, like, nobody really needs it.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:53.480
And, like, say you and I are playing together, and we both have this resource, it becomes a race to the bottom.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:18:02.340
And this is where I think it could be, like, much more interesting to play multiplayer, because the computer kind of just, like, does, like, whatever one pre-programmed thing.

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:04.920
It's like, okay, we need this, they have this, we're going to go here and stop.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.380
But I could see it being, like, more auction-like.

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:13.540
If it's like, yeah, all right, I'm going to come down to $20, and someone's, like, about to come up, and you're like, well, I'm going to go down to $19.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:15.520
And it's like, okay, now they're going to hit you first.

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:18.480
So I'm going to go down to $18, and it's interesting.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.180
It could be interesting for both sides in that way.

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:29.800
And it's just, what a unique and interesting way to present this, too, with these, like, little aliens walking down the screen and walking up the screen.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:40.080
Yeah, I really appreciate the line bars, because that is, like, something that I wouldn't, like, if they would have presented it in a different way, right?

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:44.560
If it would have been, like, oh, you press the A button to bid up, right?

00:18:44.660 --> 00:18:47.000
Like, actually holding signs, right?

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:48.660
And increasing your amounts.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.700
And the highest bidder gets whatever the person offered.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:55.040
Like, I don't think I would have came up with this, you know?

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:58.480
Whereas, like, I could come up with the inferior one.

00:18:58.480 --> 00:19:04.260
This is a very smart way, you know, albeit pretty boring for kids.

00:19:04.460 --> 00:19:12.220
This is a smart way to visually represent, like, coming together on a trade agreement, okay?

